    "result_text": "The full suite is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Change: structured address form for owners\n\n**Behavior delivered**\n- The request now accepts structured `addressLine1` (optional), `addressLine2` (optional), alongside the existing `city` and `postcode`.\n- The flat `address` field stays accepted (backward compatible), so earlier minimal owners still work.\n- An owner is valid when it supplies **either** a non-blank `addressLine1` **or** the flat `address`, plus `city`; supplying neither is rejected with a 400.\n- Normalization (trim, collapse whitespace, upper-case, `ST\u2192STREET`/`RD\u2192ROAD`/`AVE\u2192AVENUE`) applies to whichever fields are supplied, preferring the structured fields.\n- The stored/returned `address` is the composed string: normalized `addressLine1`, plus a single space and normalized `addressLine2` when `addressLine2` is present; otherwise the normalized flat address.\n\n**Why the structural impact stayed low.** The address logic lives almost entirely in one small, cohesive function (`NormalizeOwnerAddress`, WMC_other \u2248 0), so its rewrite is cheap despite its branching. I deliberately avoided growing any large class: the only edit to the big `Owner` entity was a single field plus two one-line accessors, and I did **not** touch `EnsureOwnerPostcode`, `HouseholdId`, `Locality`, or `EnsureUniqueOwnerHousehold` \u2014 they already read `city`/`postcode` (top-level in both forms), so they correctly use the structured values with no change. The either/or rejection is a self-contained new exception + handler pair (each WMC_other = 0), matching the existing house pattern rather than bloating a step.\n\n**Files touched**\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` to `OwnerFields`; removed `address` from `required` (DTOs regenerate from this).\n- `model/Owner.java` \u2014 added persisted `addressLine1` (field + accessors) so GET returns the normalized value.\n- `rest/function/owner/NormalizeOwnerAddress.java` \u2014 normalize/compose/validate.\n- `rest/escalation/InvalidAddressException.java` + `InvalidAddressExceptionHandler.java` + its escalation YAML \u2014 the 400 for a missing address.\n- `db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql` \u2014 new `address_line_1` column; `db/hsqldb/data.sql` seed inserts updated (they use positional `VALUES`, so the column count had to match \u2014 the other DBs use column-list inserts and needed no data change).\n\nAll tests pass (`Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, `BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\nOne thing worth flagging: `addressLine2` is captured and used to compose `address` but is **not** persisted or echoed back as a separate response field (only `addressLine1` and the composed `address` are). That matches the spec and tests as written; if you also want `addressLine2` round-tripped in the GET response, say so and I'll add it (one more entity field + column).",
